  "body": "If a website does:\n\n``` js\n// plays `video`\nvideo.pause();\nvar promise = video.play();\n```\n\n`promise` will be rejected because:\n- when `pause()` is called\n  - the `paused flag` will be set to `true`\n  - a task to reject the play promises will be queued (there is no promise at that point)\n- when `play()` is called\n  - a new promise is added to the play promise list\n  - the `paused flag` is set back to `false`\n  - because `readyState` is  >= `HAVE_FUTURE_DATA`, a task is queue to resolve the play promises\n\nAs a result, the video is played but the promise is rejected because the first task is to reject the promise that was just added.\n\nI think the best way to solve this is to pass the `media element's list of play promises` to the `resolve play promises` and `reject play promises` algorithm. That way, anything added after the tasks are queued will have no effect. In other word, it will prevent queued tasks to resolve promises that were created between the task was queued and the task was actually run.\n\n@foolip WDYT?\n",
